\f(CRexternal_bar\fP is an exact object. All three fields are required: \f(CRmode\fP is \f(CRoff\fP, \f(CRmain\fP or \f(CRall\fP, while \f(CRtop_padding\fP and \f(CRbottom_padding\fP are mathematical integers in \f(CRINT_MIN...INT_MAX\fP.
.sp
The native \f(CRwindow_shadow\fP, \f(CRwindow_opacity\fP, \f(CRwindow_opacity_duration\fP, \f(CRactive_window_opacity\fP and \f(CRnormal_window_opacity\fP settings use the scripting addition when yabai applies them to windows. The scripting addition requires partial SIP disablement. Other native settings do not gain that requirement merely by being stored in JSONC; \f(CRwindow_animation_duration\fP has the separate Screen Recording and SIP preflight described above.
.SS "Space policy"
.sp
Each \f(CRspaces\fP entry is an exact object with required \f(CRmatch\fP and at least one of \f(CRsettings\fP or \f(CRscaffold\fP. \f(CRmatch\fP contains exactly one selector: a positive global one\-based Mission Control \f(CRindex\fP, or a nonempty exact \f(CRuuid\fP. When present, \f(CRsettings\fP must contain at least one of \f(CRlayout\fP, \f(CRtop_padding\fP, \f(CRbottom_padding\fP, \f(CRleft_padding\fP, \f(CRright_padding\fP, \f(CRwindow_gap\fP, \f(CRaccordion_padding\fP, \f(CRsplit_type\fP and \f(CRauto_balance\fP, using the types and ranges above.
.sp
Yabai evaluates Space entries in file order and resolves each setting separately. Later matching entries win within the native Space layer. An index follows its Mission Control slot; a UUID follows the captured identity when macOS remaps its runtime Space ID. System and native\-fullscreen Spaces remain floating.
.sp
Effective precedence is:
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
built\-in < native global < native Space < runtime global < runtime Space
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.sp
A successful imperative \f(CRconfig\fP command owns the field at its runtime layer. Runtime ownership survives topology reconciliation, Space remapping, mode activation and mode deactivation, then clears only after a successful JSONC reload. When scaffolds are installed, successful imperative global or scoped layout changes request coalesced reconciliation so BSP scaffold state is restored after stack\-to\-BSP without changing inventory or runtime ownership. \f(CRspace \-\-layout\fP uses runtime Space ownership. For \f(CRwindow_focus_fallback\fP, this means \f(CRyabai \-m config window_focus_fallback recent\fP overrides the native value until a successful reload clears runtime ownership and reapplies the native candidate; omitting it from that candidate restores the built\-in \f(CRoff\fP value. Imperative \f(CRspace \-\-padding\fP, \f(CRspace \-\-gap\fP and their toggles do not create declarative ownership; a later native reconciliation may overwrite their numeric result.
.SS "Named modes"
.sp
Version 2 modes have unique nonempty UTF\-8 names and overlay base Space policies in source order. Every policy requires match and at least settings or scaffold. A scaffold omitted from a settings policy inherits; an explicit empty array clears it. Missing applications leave scaffold slots vacant and are never launched. \f(CRactive_mode\fP selects startup and reload state. Runtime activation and deactivation preserve imperative overrides; deactivation selects base policies only. Unknown names, malformed internal state, and invariant/admission/commit failures are terminal; unavailable means no installed mode runtime; allocation/resource failures and a temporarily unavailable production Space inventory query may be retried later without reserving reconciliation; only topology settling, races, or stale identity failures reserve and wait for asynchronous runtime reconciliation. Snapshot creation is worker\-only, requires fresh or destroyed output, returns owned copies, and requires destruction. Success does not claim reconciliation is complete. Query and list output is stable escaped JSON copied from the installed model. Modes do not export, import, save, capture, undo, launch applications, or persist selection.
.SS "Managed scaffold"
.sp
An optional Space \f(CRscaffold\fP is an array of one to three app matchers. Each entry is an exact object with required nonempty \f(CRpattern\fP and optional boolean \f(CRexclude\fP, using POSIX extended regular expressions. A scaffold is active only while the effective Space layout is \f(CRbsp\fP; changing the Space to another layout suspends it until BSP becomes effective again.
.sp
The first matching scaffold entry owns a window, so overlapping expressions resolve in array order. The lowest nonzero window ID matching each present entry is its primary window. Other windows from that application and windows matching no entry remain ordinary BSP overflow windows.
.sp
Present primaries compact in declaration order. One fills the Space. Two split left and right at \f(CR0.5\fP. Three place the first on the left, the second upper\-right and the third lower\-right, with both splits at \f(CR0.5\fP. Opening order does not affect that primary topology. A primary membership change or successful scaffold generation change rebuilds the detached BSP root transactionally; overflow\-only membership changes retain normal BSP insertion and collapse behavior. During a required rebuild, overflow windows are reinserted in captured order through the ordinary BSP insertion policy.
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
{
  "version": 1,
  "spaces": [
    {
      "match": { "index": 2 },
      "scaffold": [
        { "pattern": "^Mail$" },
        { "pattern": "^Calendar$" },
        { "pattern": "^Messages$" }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.SS "Accordion geometry"
.sp
\f(CRaccordion_horizontal\fP and \f(CRaccordion_vertical\fP are managed layouts. Every managed window remains in the Space forest and managed\-window table; rules with \f(CRmanage=false\fP, native\-fullscreen Spaces and system Spaces remain outside that forest. Accordion does not require the scripting addition.
.sp
The layout keeps one stable membership order. Every window retains the same\-size overlapping frame; changing focus raises that window above its peers without changing geometry or moving it in the membership order. Insertion, removal, minimization, restoration, a cross\-Space move or an explicit swap may change membership order.
.sp
After display, menu bar, Dock, external\-bar and Space padding constraints, let \f(CRL\fP be the finite nonnegative length on the accordion axis, \f(CRn\fP the managed\-window count and \f(CRp\fP the nonnegative \f(CRaccordion_padding\fP request. With no windows, yabai emits no frames. One window receives the complete usable area. For two or more windows, the effective exposed strip is \f(CRs = min(p, L / n)\fP and every window receives axis length \f(CRW = L \- (n \- 1) * s\fP. Window \f(CRi\fP in stable zero\-based order starts at \f(CRi * s\fP from the usable\-area origin. In ideal real\-number geometry, the equal frames overlap, remain inside the usable area, fill the cross axis and place their first and last edges on the constrained boundaries. Stored origins are deterministically rounded and clamped to representable in\-bounds coordinates, while every stored frame uses the same rounded axis length; at extreme coordinate magnitudes, adjacent origins may therefore coincide. Over\-capacity requests give every window one equal share rather than a negative or nonfinite frame. A request of zero is valid and makes all frames coincide.
.sp
Focus, inventory, delayed create, destroy, minimize, restore, Space move, display change, topology reconciliation and Mission Control exit paths recompute the same geometry. A successful JSONC reload may add, change or remove the layout and \f(CRaccordion_padding\fP under the normal transaction and precedence rules. Preparation failure preserves the installed generation, roots, managed\-window table, ownership and effective layout.
.sp
To assign Mission Control Space 9 to the horizontal accordion layout with the default strip request:
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
{
  "version": 1,
  "spaces": [
    {
      "match": { "index": 9 },
      "settings": {
        "layout": "accordion_horizontal",
        "accordion_padding": 30
      }
    }
  ]
}
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.SS "Native rules"
.sp
Each \f(CRrules\fP entry accepts exactly \f(CRlabel\fP, \f(CRone_shot\fP, \f(CRmatch\fP and \f(CReffects\fP. \f(CRmatch\fP is required and nonempty. \f(CRlabel\fP is optional, nonempty, unique within the file and at most 1,024 UTF\-8 bytes. \f(CRone_shot\fP is an optional boolean, defaulting to \f(CRfalse\fP. \f(CReffects\fP is optional and may be empty, so an effectless persistent rule is valid.
.sp
\f(CRmatch\fP accepts \f(CRapp\fP, \f(CRtitle\fP, \f(CRrole\fP and \f(CRsubrole\fP. Each matcher is an exact object with required nonempty \f(CRpattern\fP and optional boolean \f(CRexclude\fP, which defaults to \f(CRfalse\fP. Patterns use POSIX extended regular expressions and are compiled during schema validation. A rule may use role or subrole without app or title.
.sp
\f(CReffects\fP accepts:
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
\f(CRdisplay\fP: exact object \f(CR{ "index": n }\fP, where \f(CRn\fP is \f(CR1...INT_MAX\fP;
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
\f(CRspace\fP: exact object \f(CR{ "index": n }\fP, where \f(CRn\fP is \f(CR1...INT_MAX\fP;
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
\f(CRfollow\fP: boolean shared by the selected display or Space target. It is invalid without either target;
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
\f(CRgrid\fP: exactly \f(CR[rows, columns, x, y, width, height]\fP. Each value is a mathematical integer in \f(CR0...UINT_MAX\fP; rows and columns must be greater than zero;
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
\f(CRopacity\fP: finite float in \f(CR0...1\fP;
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
\f(CRmanage\fP, \f(CRsticky\fP, \f(CRmouse_follows_focus\fP, \f(CRnative_fullscreen\fP: boolean;
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
\f(CRsub_layer\fP: \f(CRbelow\fP, \f(CRnormal\fP, \f(CRabove\fP, \f(CRauto\fP;
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
\f(CRscratchpad\fP: nonempty string of at most 1,024 UTF\-8 bytes. The reserved identifiers \f(CRfloat\fP, \f(CRsticky\fP, \f(CRshadow\fP, \f(CRsplit\fP, \f(CRzoom\-parent\fP, \f(CRzoom\-fullscreen\fP, \f(CRwindowed\-fullscreen\fP, \f(CRnative\-fullscreen\fP, \f(CRexpose\fP, \f(CRpip\fP and \f(CRrecover\fP are rejected.
.RE
.sp
A rule cannot specify both \f(CRscratchpad\fP and \f(CRmanage\fP. If both display and Space are present, Space wins and the shared \f(CRfollow\fP applies to it.
.sp
The native \f(CRsticky\fP, \f(CRsub_layer\fP and \f(CRopacity\fP effects call the scripting addition and require partial SIP disablement. Assigning a \f(CRscratchpad\fP label is retained by yabai, but normal scratchpad ordering, hiding, recovery and presentation use scripting\-addition operations and therefore need the same setup. The other native rule effects do not require the scripting addition solely because they appear in JSONC.
.sp
Native display and Space targets use one\-based indices only. Display indices resolve using the candidate \f(CRdisplay_arrangement_order\fP; equal coordinates use the captured managed\-display ordinal as the final tie\-break. Space indices use the captured global Mission Control order. Runtime preparation strictly rejects malformed, incomplete, duplicate, zero\-ID or ambiguous display/Space topology instead of filtering it.
.sp
Some macOS topologies expose a user Space whose public UUID is the empty string. Yabai preserves that empty value in query output and it cannot match a declarative UUID selector. Internally, index policy and native index rules use a private fallback identity containing the parent display UUID, display ID and managed ordinal, Space ID and kind, and global Mission Control ordinal. The fallback is never serialized as a public UUID. If any tuple field changes, the installed target becomes stale; yabai does not transfer a UUID\-less view, tree or runtime ownership to another Space ID. A coincidentally reused complete tuple cannot be distinguished because macOS supplied no durable UUID.
.sp
Topology reconciliation publishes a fully prepared current\-topology rule context even when fallback\-identity drift prevents safe Space\-policy or forest publication. Native rule targets therefore become stale immediately, while the existing Space roots, managed\-window table and runtime ownership remain unchanged. Yabai does not guess or transfer that state. A coherent Space lifecycle event, configuration reload or daemon restart may be required to construct fresh UUID\-less view state.
.sp
Targets resolve once per configuration generation to an internal ID and an identity fingerprint. A later topology change does not retarget a native rule. When the ID disappears, changes kind, maps to another UUID, or changes a UUID\-less fallback tuple, that rule contributes no move or follow effect, still contributes its independent effects, and emits at most one warning for that rule and generation. A stale Space target does not fall back to a display target in the same rule. A later valid runtime target may still win during rule folding.
.sp
Native rules remain before runtime rules. Runtime \f(CRrule \-\-add\fP cannot claim a native label, even after a native one\-shot has been consumed, and \f(CRrule \-\-remove\fP cannot remove a native rule. A successful reload recreates native one\-shots and retains runtime rules in their existing order. Direct reapply and delayed window\-created retries fold against the currently installed generation context. Reload changes registrations for future windows; it does not undo or reapply effects on existing windows. Use \f(CRrule \-\-apply\fP for an explicit reapplication where supported.
.SS "Reload, topology and atomicity"
.sp
The reload grammar is exactly:
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
yabai \-m config reload
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.sp
A scoped form, trailing token or mixed lifecycle/setting message is rejected before mutation. Reload uses the installed absolute lexical path. When generation is 0 because startup found no native file, it repeats default discovery. A successful first installation uses generation 1; each later successful reload increments once, including a byte\-identical reload. Reload at \f(CRUINT64_MAX\fP is rejected.
.sp
Reload parses and prepares the entire candidate before changing installed state. Preparation includes regular expressions, strict topology, target fingerprints, runtime\-label collision checks, permission preflight, detached Space trees, and a complete managed\-window table. Any preparation failure preserves the installed model, path, generation, settings, runtime ownership, native rules, views, forest and managed\-window table.
.sp
The event\-loop worker publishes prepared settings, views, forest, managed\-window table, native rule prefix, native label index, model, path, fold context and generation through a no\-allocation, no\-post boundary. Allocation\-capable refreshes, tree flushes, animations, notifications and logging run afterward as best\-effort reconciliation. A failure after publication does not roll back the committed generation.
.sp
Topology lifecycle work uses a binary reservation before queue admission. Duplicate triggers coalesce. The event handler clears the reservation on entry so a trigger during processing can queue one follow\-up. Queue admission owns event context and daemon response/socket cleanup on both acceptance and rejection.
.sp
Focus follows mouse uses one daemon\-lifetime event tap with the superset event mask. \f(CRconfigured_ffm_mode\fP holds the configured value. Nested menu tracking forces the atomic effective mode to \f(CRoff\fP; closing the final menu derives the effective mode from the latest configured value rather than restoring a saved stale value.
.SS "Diagnostics"
.sp
Startup and offline\-check errors go to stderr; reload errors go to the daemon client. Configuration errors and stale native targets also request a best\-effort Notification Center notification and write an error to the macOS unified log with subsystem \f(CRcom.asmvik.yabai\fP and category \f(CRconfiguration\fP.
.sp
A complete stderr or notification diagnostic is capped at 2,048 UTF\-8 bytes. A failing daemon response has the same 2,048\-byte wire cap, including its one\-byte failure marker, so its diagnostic text is at most 2,047 bytes. The dynamic unified\-log payload is capped at 1,024 UTF\-8 bytes. Truncation preserves valid UTF\-8. Paths escape control characters, JSON paths are sanitized, and diagnostics omit source text, candidate values, regular\-expression text, shell commands and unknown key text. Notification and log sink failures do not replace the primary error. Notification presentation and unified\-log persistence remain subject to macOS settings and policy.
.sp
Inspect recent configuration log records with:
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
log show \-\-predicate \*(Aqsubsystem == "com.asmvik.yabai" && category == "configuration"\*(Aq \-\-last 5m
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.SH "DEFINITIONS"
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
REGEX\&       := \c
.URL "https://www.gnu.org/software/findutils/manual/html_node/find_html/posix_002dextended\-regular\-expression\-syntax.html" "POSIX extended regular expression syntax" ""

LABEL\&       := arbitrary string/text used as an identifier

LAYER\&       := below | normal | above | auto

BOOL_SEL\&    := on | off

FLOAT_SEL\&   := 0 < <value> <= 1.0

RULE_SEL\&    := <index> | LABEL

SIGNAL_SEL\&  := <index> | LABEL

DIR_SEL\&     := north | east | south | west

STACK_SEL\&   := stack.prev | stack.next | stack.first | stack.last | stack.recent | stack.<index (1\-based)>

WINDOW_SEL\&  := prev | next | first | last | recent | mouse | largest | smallest | sibling | first_nephew | second_nephew | uncle | first_cousin | second_cousin | STACK_SEL | DIR_SEL | <window id>

DISPLAY_SEL := prev | next | first | last | recent | mouse | DIR_SEL | <arrangement index (1\-based)> | LABEL

SPACE_SEL\&   := prev | next | first | last | recent | mouse | <mission\-control index (1\-based)> | LABEL

EASING\&      := ease_in_sine\&  | ease_out_sine\&  | ease_in_out_sine\&  |
               ease_in_quad\&  | ease_out_quad\&  | ease_in_out_quad\&  |
               ease_in_cubic | ease_out_cubic | ease_in_out_cubic |
               ease_in_quart | ease_out_quart | ease_in_out_quart |
               ease_in_quint | ease_out_quint | ease_in_out_quint |
               ease_in_expo\&  | ease_out_expo\&  | ease_in_out_expo\&  |
               ease_in_circ\&  | ease_out_circ\&  | ease_in_out_circ
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.SH "DOMAINS"
.SS "Mode"
.sp
yabai \-m mode \-\-activate \fI<name>\fP
.RS 4
Select exactly one declared mode. Unknown names are terminal; other failures advise retry after asynchronous reconciliation settles.
.RE
.sp
yabai \-m mode \-\-deactivate
.RS 4
Select base policies only and preserve imperative runtime overrides.
.RE
.sp
yabai \-m mode \-\-query
.RS 4
Print the active mode as an escaped JSON string or null.
.RE
.sp
yabai \-m mode \-\-list
.RS 4
Print declared names in source order and the active name or null as valid JSON. Mode commands reject missing, extra and trailing tokens.
.RE
.SS "Config"
.SS "General Syntax"
.sp
yabai \-m config <global setting>
.RS 4
Get or set the value of <global setting>.
.RE
.sp
yabai \-m config [\-\-space \fI<SPACE_SEL>\fP] <space setting>
.RS 4
Get or set the value of <space setting>.
.RE
.sp
yabai \-m config reload
.RS 4
Reload the native JSONC configuration. The command must be unscoped and contain no trailing token or additional config mutation.
.RE
.SS "Global Settings"
.sp
\fBdebug_output\fP [\fI<BOOL_SEL>\fP]
.RS 4
Enable output of debug information to stdout.
.RE
.sp
\fBexternal_bar\fP [\fI<main|all|off>:<top_padding>:<bottom_padding>\fP]
.RS 4
Specify top and bottom padding for a potential custom bar that you may be running.
.br
\fImain\fP: Apply the given padding only to spaces located on the main display.
.br
\fIall\fP:  Apply the given padding to all spaces regardless of their display.
.br
\fIoff\fP:  Do not apply any special padding.
.RE
.sp
\fBmenubar\fP [\fI<BOOL_SEL>\fP]
.RS 4
Show yabai Spaces as a read\-only native macOS menu bar status item. Default is \fIoff\fP.
.br
The indicator works with full System Integrity Protection (SIP) enabled and follows the system menu bar visibility, including autohide, and \fBmenubar_opacity\fP settings.
.RE
.sp
\fBmenubar_style\fP [\fIcompact|expanded\fP]
.RS 4
Set the menu bar status item layout. Default is \fIexpanded\fP.
.br
\fIcompact\fP: Show one screen outline for the keyboard\-focused display\(cqs visible space.
.br
\fIexpanded\fP: Show one screen outline for every filtered space, grouped by display, in the menu bar.
.RE
.sp
\fBmenubar_space_filter\fP [\fIoccupied|all\fP]
.RS 4
Select which spaces are shown by the menu bar item and its menu. Default is \fIoccupied\fP.
.br
\fIoccupied\fP: Show spaces with user\-facing application windows, spaces that are currently visible on a display, and spaces with unresolved occupancy. Floating, unmanaged, minimized, hidden and native\-fullscreen application windows count. Sticky\-only, system and transient windows do not make a space occupied.
.br
\fIall\fP: Show all user\-navigation spaces reported by macOS, including native\-fullscreen spaces. Virtual spaces are not created or shown.
.br
The compact and expanded layouts share the same display\-grouped informational menu. A checkmark marks each display\(cqs visible space; the focused display is the display with keyboard focus and is tracked separately from visible spaces. Space and display rows never focus or switch Spaces; clicking the expanded indicator opens the menu. Layout and filter choices remain interactive and change the running configuration only; set these options in \fByabairc\fP to persist them.
.RE
.sp
\fBmenubar_opacity\fP [\fI<FLOAT_SEL>\fP]
.RS 4
Changes the transparency of the macOS menubar.
.br
If the value is 0.0, the menubar will no longer respond to mouse\-events, effectively hiding the menubar permanently.
.br
The menubar will automatically become fully opaque upon entering a native\-fullscreen space, and adjusted down afterwards.
.RE
.sp
\fBmouse_follows_focus\fP [\fI<BOOL_SEL>\fP]
.RS 4
When focusing a window, put the mouse at its center.
.RE
.sp
\fBfocus_follows_mouse\fP [\fIautofocus|autoraise|off\fP]
.RS 4
Automatically focus the window under the mouse.
.RE
.sp
\fBdisplay_arrangement_order\fP [\fIdefault|vertical|horizontal\fP]
.RS 4
Specify how displays are ordered (determined by center point).
.br
\fIdefault\fP: Native macOS ordering.
.br
\fIvertical\fP: Order by y\-coordinate (followed by x\-coordinate when equal).
.br
\fIhorizontal\fP: Order by x\-coordinate (followed by y\-coordinate when equal).
.RE
.sp
\fBwindow_origin_display\fP [\fIdefault|focused|cursor\fP]
.RS 4
Specify which display a newly created window should be managed in.
.br
\fIdefault\fP: The display in which the window is created (standard macOS behaviour).
.br
\fIfocused\fP: The display that has focus when the window is created.
.br
\fIcursor\fP: The display that currently holds the mouse cursor.
.RE
.sp
\fBwindow_focus_fallback\fP [\fIoff|recent\fP]
.RS 4
Select the native policy used after \f(CRWINDOW_DESTROYED\fP, \f(CRWINDOW_MINIMIZED\fP and \f(CRAPPLICATION_HIDDEN\fP. The default is \fIoff\fP.
.br
\fIrecent\fP: After the triggering handler finishes mutating manager state, queue one event\-loop follow\-up. When that follow\-up executes, yabai rechecks the current focused window and does nothing if an eligible window is already focused. Otherwise it resolves the same valid managed window as \f(CRwindow \-\-focus recent\fP and focuses it through the normal focus path. Destroyed, minimized, hidden, ineligible, invalid and unavailable recent windows are ignored; no candidate is a no\-op. Duplicate triggers coalesce while one follow\-up is pending.
.br
This is a typed focus policy. It does not execute generic signals, invoke a shell, spawn a process, or route a synthetic command through the daemon socket. Existing user\-configured signals keep their normal behavior and order.
.RE
.sp
\fBwindow_placement\fP [\fIfirst_child|second_child\fP]
.RS 4
Specify whether managed windows should become the first or second leaf\-node.
.RE
.sp
\fBwindow_insertion_point\fP [\fIfocused|first|last\fP]
.RS 4
Specify where new managed windows will be inserted.
.RE
.sp
\fBwindow_zoom_persist\fP [\fI<BOOL_SEL>\fP]
.RS 4
Windows will keep their zoom\-state through layout changes.
.RE
.sp
\fBskip_window_focus_animation\fP [\fI<BOOL_SEL>\fP]
.RS 4
Bypass macOS space animation when an application or window on an inactive space is focused.
.br
You should only enable this if you do NOT use the scripting addition.
.br
The scripting addition implements the same functionality in a more robust way.
.RE
.sp
\fBwindow_shadow\fP [\fI<BOOL_SEL>|float\fP]
.RS 4
Draw shadow for windows.
.br
System Integrity Protection must be partially disabled.
.RE
.sp
\fBwindow_opacity\fP [\fI<BOOL_SEL>\fP]
.RS 4
Enable opacity for windows.
.br
System Integrity Protection must be partially disabled.
.RE
.sp
\fBwindow_opacity_duration\fP [\fI<FLOAT_SEL>\fP]
.RS 4
Duration of transition between active / normal opacity.
.br
System Integrity Protection must be partially disabled.
.RE
.sp
\fBactive_window_opacity\fP [\fI<FLOAT_SEL>\fP]
.RS 4
Opacity of the focused window.
.br
System Integrity Protection must be partially disabled.
.RE
.sp
\fBnormal_window_opacity\fP [\fI<FLOAT_SEL>\fP]
.RS 4
Opacity of an unfocused window.
.br
System Integrity Protection must be partially disabled.
.RE
.sp
\fBwindow_animation_duration\fP [\fI<FLOAT_SEL>\fP]
.RS 4
Duration of window frame animation.
.br
If 0.0, the change in dimension is not animated.
.br
Requires Screen Recording permissions.
.br
System Integrity Protection must be partially disabled.
.RE
.sp
\fBwindow_animation_easing\fP [\fI<EASING>\fP]
.RS 4
Easing function to use for window animations.
.br
See \c
.URL "https://easings.net" "" ""
for details.
.RE
.sp
\fBinsert_feedback_color\fP [\fI0xAARRGGBB\fP]
.RS 4
Color of the \fBwindow \-\-insert\fP message and mouse_drag selection.
.br
The purpose is to provide a visual preview of the new window frame.
.RE
.sp
\fBinsert_feedback_radius\fP [\fIfloat\fP]
.RS 4
Corner radius in display points for insert feedback. Must be finite and greater than or equal to zero. (default: 9)
.RE
.sp
\fBinsert_feedback_width\fP [\fIfloat\fP]
.RS 4
Stroke width in display points for insert feedback. Must be finite and greater than zero.
.br
Rendering clamps the width to half the shorter feedback\-frame dimension. (default: 2)
.RE
.sp
\fBsplit_ratio\fP [\fI<FLOAT_SEL>\fP]
.RS 4
Specify the size distribution when a window is split.
.RE
.sp
\fBmouse_modifier\fP [\fIcmd|alt|shift|ctrl|fn\fP]
.RS 4
Keyboard modifier used for moving and resizing windows.
.RE
.sp
\fBmouse_action1\fP [\fImove|resize\fP]
.RS 4
Action performed when pressing \fImouse_modifier\fP + \fIbutton1\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fBmouse_action2\fP [\fImove|resize\fP]
.RS 4
Action performed when pressing \fImouse_modifier\fP + \fIbutton2\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fBmouse_drop_action\fP [\fIswap|stack\fP]
.RS 4
Action performed when a bsp\-managed window is dropped in the center of some other bsp\-managed window.
.RE
.SS "Space Settings"
.sp
\fBlayout\fP [\fIbsp|stack|float|accordion_horizontal|accordion_vertical\fP]
.RS 4
Set the layout of the selected space. \f(CRaccordion_horizontal\fP arranges the strips along the x\-axis; \f(CRaccordion_vertical\fP arranges them along the y\-axis.
.RE
.sp
\fBsplit_type\fP [\fIvertical|horizontal|auto\fP]
.RS 4
Specify how a window should be split.
.br
\fIvertical\fP: The window is split along the y\-axis.
.br
\fIhorizontal\fP: The window is split along the x\-axis.
.br
\fIauto\fP: The axis is determined based on width/height ratio.
.RE
.sp
\fBtop_padding\fP [\fI<integer number>\fP]
.RS 4
Padding added at the upper side of the selected space.
.RE
.sp
\fBbottom_padding\fP [\fI<integer number>\fP]
.RS 4
Padding added at the lower side of the selected space.
.RE
.sp
\fBleft_padding\fP [\fI<integer number>\fP]
.RS 4
Padding added at the left side of the selected space.
.RE
.sp
\fBright_padding\fP [\fI<integer number>\fP]
.RS 4
Padding added at the right side of the selected space.
.RE
.sp
\fBwindow_gap\fP [\fI<integer number>\fP]
.RS 4
Size of the gap that separates windows for the selected space.
.RE
.sp
\fBaccordion_padding\fP [\fI<nonnegative integer number>\fP]
.RS 4
Requested exposed strip size for accordion layouts. The default is 30 display points. This setting has no effect on \f(CRbsp\fP, \f(CRstack\fP or \f(CRfloat\fP geometry.
.RE
.sp
\fBauto_balance\fP [\fI<BOOL_SEL>|x\-axis|y\-axis\fP]
.RS 4
Balance the window tree upon change, so that all windows occupy an equally sized area.
.RE
.SS "Display"
.SS "General Syntax"
.sp
yabai \-m display [\fI<DISPLAY_SEL\fP>] \fI<COMMAND>\fP
.SS "COMMAND"
.sp
\fB\-\-focus\fP \fI<DISPLAY_SEL>\fP
.RS 4
Focus the given display.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-space\fP \fI<SPACE_SEL>\fP
.RS 4
The given space will become visible on the selected display, without changing focus.
.br
The given space must belong to the selected display.
.br
System Integrity Protection must be partially disabled.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-label\fP [\fI<LABEL>\fP]
.RS 4
Label the selected display, allowing that label to be used as an alias in commands that take a \f(CRDISPLAY_SEL\fP parameter.
.br
If the command is called without an argument it will try to remove a previously assigned label.
.RE
.SS "Space"
.SS "General Syntax"
.sp
yabai \-m space [\fI<SPACE_SEL>\fP] \fI<COMMAND>\fP
.SS "COMMAND"
.sp
\fB\-\-focus\fP \fI<SPACE_SEL>\fP
.RS 4
Focus the given space.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-switch\fP \fI<SPACE_SEL>\fP
.RS 4
The selected space will always be the currently focused space.
.br
The given space substitutes the selected space, gaining focus.
.br
If the selected space and the given space belong to different displays, this behaves like \fI\-\-swap\fP.
.br
If the selected space and the given space belong to the same display, this behaves like \fI\-\-focus\fP.
.br
System Integrity Protection must be partially disabled.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-create\fP  [\fI<DISPLAY_SEL>\fP]
.RS 4
Create a new space on the given display.
.br
If none specified, use the display of the active space instead.
.br
System Integrity Protection must be partially disabled.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-destroy\fP [\fI<SPACE_SEL>\fP]
.RS 4
Remove the given space.
.br
If none specified, use the selected space instead.
.br
System Integrity Protection must be partially disabled.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-move\fP \fI<SPACE_SEL>\fP
.RS 4
Move position of the selected space to the position of the given space.
.br
The selected space and given space must both belong to the same display.
.br
System Integrity Protection must be partially disabled.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-swap\fP \fI<SPACE_SEL>\fP
.RS 4
Swap the selected space with the given space.
.br
If the selected space and given space belong to different displays, all the windows will swap.
.br
If the selected space and given space belong to the same display, the actual spaces will swap.
.br
System Integrity Protection must be partially disabled.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-display\fP \fI<DISPLAY_SEL>\fP
.RS 4
Send the selected space to the given display.
.br
System Integrity Protection must be partially disabled.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-equalize\fP [\fIx\-axis|y\-axis\fP]
.RS 4
Reset the split ratios on the selected space to the default value along the given axis.
.br
If no axis is specified, use both.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-balance\fP [\fIx\-axis|y\-axis\fP]
.RS 4
Adjust the split ratios on the selected space so that all windows along the given axis occupy the same area.
.br
If no axis is specified, use both.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-mirror\fP \fIx\-axis|y\-axis\fP
.RS 4
Flip the tree of the selected space along the given axis.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-rotate\fP \fI90|180|270\fP
.RS 4
Rotate the tree of the selected space.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-padding\fP \fIabs|rel:<top>:<bottom>:<left>:<right>\fP
.RS 4
Padding added at the sides of the selected space.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-gap\fP \fIabs|rel:<gap>\fP
.RS 4
Size of the gap that separates windows on the selected space.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-toggle\fP \fIpadding|gap|mission\-control|show\-desktop\fP
.RS 4
Toggle space setting on or off for the selected space.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-layout\fP \fIbsp|stack|float|accordion_horizontal|accordion_vertical\fP
.RS 4
Set the layout of the selected space.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-label\fP [\fI<LABEL>\fP]
.RS 4
Label the selected space, allowing that label to be used as an alias in commands that take a \f(CRSPACE_SEL\fP parameter.
.br
If the command is called without an argument it will try to remove a previously assigned label.
.RE
.SS "Window"
.SS "General Syntax"
.sp
yabai \-m window [\fI<WINDOW_SEL>\fP] \fI<COMMAND>\fP
.SS "COMMAND"
.sp
\fB\-\-focus\fP [\fI<WINDOW_SEL>\fP]
.RS 4
Focus the given window.
.br
If none specified, focus the selected window instead.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-close\fP [\fI<WINDOW_SEL>\fP]
.RS 4
Close the given window.
.br
If none specified, close the selected window instead.
.br
Only works on windows that provide a close button in its titlebar.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-minimize\fP [\fI<WINDOW_SEL>\fP]
.RS 4
Minimize the given window.
.br
If none specified, minimize the selected window instead.
.br
Only works on windows that provide a minimize button in its titlebar.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-deminimize\fP \fI<WINDOW_SEL>\fP
.RS 4
Restore the given window if it is minimized.
.br
The window will only get focus if the owning application has focus.
.br
Note that you can also \fI\-\-focus\fP a minimized window to restore it as the focused window.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-display\fP \fI<DISPLAY_SEL>\fP
.RS 4
Send the selected window to the given display.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-space\fP \fI<SPACE_SEL>\fP
.RS 4
Send the selected window to the given space.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-swap\fP \fI<WINDOW_SEL>\fP
.RS 4
Swap position of the selected window and the given window.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-warp\fP \fI<WINDOW_SEL>\fP
.RS 4
Re\-insert the selected window, splitting the given window.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-stack\fP \fI<WINDOW_SEL>\fP
.RS 4
Stack the given window on top of the selected window.
.br
Any kind of warp operation performed on a stacked window will unstack it.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-insert\fP \fI<DIR_SEL>|stack\fP
.RS 4
Set the splitting mode of the selected window.
.br
If the current splitting mode matches the selected mode, the action will be undone.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-grid\fP \fI<rows>:<cols>:<start\-x>:<start\-y>:<width>:<height>\fP
.RS 4
Set the frame of the selected window based on a self\-defined grid.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-move\fP \fIabs|rel:<dx>:<dy>\fP
.RS 4
If type is \fIrel\fP the selected window is moved by \fIdx\fP pixels horizontally and \fIdy\fP pixels vertically.
.br
If type is \fIabs\fP \fIdx\fP and \fIdy\fP will become the new position.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-resize\fP \fItop|left|bottom|right|top_left|top_right|bottom_right|bottom_left|abs:<dx>:<dy>\fP
.RS 4
Resize the selected window by moving the given handle \fIdx\fP pixels horizontally and \fIdy\fP pixels vertically.
.br
If handle is \fIabs\fP the new size will be \fIdx\fP width and \fIdy\fP height and cannot be used on managed windows.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-ratio\fP \fIrel|abs:<dr>\fP
.RS 4
If type is \fIrel\fP the split ratio of the selected window is changed by \fIdr\fP, otherwise \fIdr\fP will become the new split ratio.
.br
A positive/negative delta will increase/decrease the size of the left\-child.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-toggle\fP \fIfloat|sticky|pip|shadow|split|zoom\-parent|zoom\-fullscreen|windowed\-fullscreen|native\-fullscreen|expose|<LABEL>\fP
.RS 4
Toggle the given property of the selected window.
.br
The following properties require System Integrity Protection to be partially disabled: sticky, pip, shadow, LABEL (scratchpad identifier) .
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-sub\-layer\fP \fI<LAYER>\fP
.RS 4
Set the stacking sub\-layer of the selected window.
.br
The window will no longer be eligible for automatic change in sub\-layer when managed/unmanaged.
.br
Specify the value \fIauto\fP to reset back to normal and make it become automatically managed.
.br
System Integrity Protection must be partially disabled.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-opacity\fP \fI<FLOAT_SEL>\fP
.RS 4
Set the opacity of the selected window.
.br
The window will no longer be eligible for automatic change in opacity upon focus change.
.br
Specify the value \fI0.0\fP to reset back to full opacity and make it become automatically managed.
.br
System Integrity Protection must be partially disabled.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-raise\fP [\fI<WINDOW_SEL>\fP]
.RS 4
Orders the selected window above the given window, or to the front within its layer.
.br
System Integrity Protection must be partially disabled.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lower\fP [\fI<WINDOW_SEL>\fP]
.RS 4
Orders the selected window below the given window, or to the back within its layer.
.br
System Integrity Protection must be partially disabled.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-scratchpad\fP [\fI<LABEL>|recover\fP]
.RS 4
Unique identifier used to identify a window scratchpad.
.br
An identifier may only be assigned to a single window at any given time.
.br
A scratchpad window will automatically be treated as a (manage=off) floating window.
.br
If the scratchpad is already taken by another window, this assignment will fail.
.br
If the scratchpad is re\-assigned, the previous identifier will become available.
.br
If no value is given, the window will seize to be a scratchpad window.
.br
The special value \fIrecover\fP can be used to forcefully bring all scratchpad windows to the front.
.br
This can be useful if windows become inaccessible due to a restart or crash.
.br
System Integrity Protection must be partially disabled.
.RE
.SS "Query"
.SS "General Syntax"
.sp
yabai \-m query \fI<COMMAND>\fP [\fI<PROPERTIES>\fP] [\fI<ARGUMENT>\fP]
.SS "COMMAND"
.sp
\fB\-\-displays\fP
.RS 4
Retrieve information about displays.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-spaces\fP
.RS 4
Retrieve information about spaces.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-windows\fP
.RS 4
Retrieve information about windows.
.RE
.SS "ARGUMENT"
.sp
\fB\-\-display\fP [\fI<DISPLAY_SEL>\fP]
.RS 4
Constrain matches to the selected display.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-space\fP [\fI<SPACE_SEL>\fP]
.RS 4
Constrain matches to the selected space.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-window\fP [\fI<WINDOW_SEL>\fP]
.RS 4
Constrain matches to the selected window.
.RE
.SS "PROPERTIES"
.sp
A comma\-separated string containing the name of fields to include in the output.
.br
The name of the provided fields must be present in the dataformat of the corresponding entity.
.SS "DATAFORMAT"
.sp
DISPLAY
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
{
    "id": number,
    "uuid": string,
    "index": number,
    "label": string,
    "frame": object {
        "x": number,
        "y": number,
        "w": number,
        "h": number
    },
    "visible\-frame": object {
        "x": number,
        "y": number,
        "w": number,
        "h": number
    },
    "spaces": array of number,
    "has\-focus": bool
}
.fam
.fi
